  "raw_text": "Step 1: Apply the AM-HM inequality to the terms \\(a, b, c, d\\).\n\nThe AM-HM inequality states that for any set of positive real numbers, the arithmetic mean is at least as large as the harmonic mean. For \\(a, b, c, d\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b + c + d}{4} \\geq \\frac{4}{\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d}}\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Rearrange the inequality to express it in terms of the product of sums.\n\nMultiplying both sides by \\(4 \\left(\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d}\\right)\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n(a + b + c + d) \\left(\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d}\\right) \\geq 16\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Check if equality can be achieved.\n\nEquality in the AM-HM inequality holds when all the terms are equal. So, let \\(a = b = c = d\\). Then,\n\n\\[\na + b + c + d = 4a \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d} = \\frac{4}{a}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n(a + b + c + d) \\left(\\frac{1}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} + \\frac{1}{c} + \\frac{1}{d}\\right) = 4a \\cdot \\frac{4}{a} = 16\n\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{16}<|im_end|>",
